cff-version: 1.2.0
message: "If you use this dataset or software, please cite it as below."
title: "Quantum Space Memory: Preregistered Cross-Job Information Persistence Tests on IBM Heron Transmons (Null Results and Erratum)"
version: "0.2.0"
date-released: "2026-11-18"
repository-code: "https://github.com/silentnoisehun/quantum-space-memory"
license: MIT
authors:
  - name: "Róbert, Máté"
    family-names: "Róbert"
    given-names: "Máté"
    affiliation: "Silent Coder Labs"
    orcid: "0009-0003-3986-6039"
abstract: >-
  Preregistered tests of whether superconducting transmon qubits retain information
  about a prior state preparation across separate execution sessions ("memory in space").
  Contains the frozen preregistrations (committed before each run), layout selection,
  raw Qiskit counts with job IDs, positive controls and analysis scripts for IBM Heron r2.

  Results: all preregistered primary tests are null. Spatial bath spectroscopy
  (ibm_kingston, job dbnc6ooqtr0c73acp00g): H0 under the preregistered rule; the largest
  deviation is z = 2.89, below the 3σ threshold (Holm-corrected minimum p = 0.057 over
  15 tests). The TLS/T2* protocol is not a valid test: its two arms compile to identical
  circuits. Primary cross-job delta: Δ = +0.00023, p = 0.36; zero-IQ ground-state centroid
  shift Δ = −0.00069, p = 0.55; energy-matched control Δ = −0.00135, p = 0.29.

  Erratum 2026-11-18: the original spectroscopy analysis printed "H1 SUPPORTED" because
  it used curve-fit errors instead of shot-noise errors; a later commit changed the
  decision rule after seeing the data. Version 0.2.0 restores the preregistered rule
  with binomial bootstrap errors. All numbers can be recomputed from the archived
  raw data with the included scripts.
keywords:
  - "quantum computing"
  - "superconducting qubits"
  - "transmon"
  - "IBM Quantum"
  - "preregistration"
  - "null result"
  - "reproducibility"
  - "non-Markovian noise"
